1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:10,470
I am Bangie anak Embol. Originally a native of Sungai Kain.

2
00:00:10,550 --> 00:00:20,220
I come from a lineage of weavers from long ago. Starting from my grandparents back then.

3
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:27,520
By the time I was 10 years old

4
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:30,990
I already knew how to weave

5
00:00:31,070 --> 00:00:33,470
and I already knew how to tie patterns (ngebat).

6
00:00:33,550 --> 00:00:36,420
From then on, when I first started tying patterns (ngebat)

7
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:40,960
I made a pua that wasn't too big,

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,920
After that, the second and third time,

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,920
I made them bigger and bigger.

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,920
Back in the early days, I learned from my grandmother,

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,270
because my mother passed away. I was an only child

12
00:00:56,350 --> 00:00:59,870
Because my mother passed away,

13
00:00:59,950 --> 00:01:02,870
I lived together with my grandmother.

14
00:01:02,950 --> 00:01:06,350
Living together with me, she was the one who taught me how to weave.

15
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:12,400
So starting from the age of 10, I have been weaving, right up until now.

16
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:18,420
Besides pua kumbu, things like plaiting: plaiting mats,

17
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:22,960
plaiting sun hats (tanggi); I can do them too

18
00:01:23,450 --> 00:01:29,300
After that, brocade weaving (nyungkit), extra-warp patterning (milih), and embroidery (nyulam).

19
00:01:29,380 --> 00:01:32,780
Beyond that, all of our traditional Iban tasks around the longhouse,

20
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:35,270
I can do every single one

21
00:01:35,350 --> 00:01:40,270
Because in my heart, even until now,

22
00:01:40,350 --> 00:01:46,490
I do not want these traditions to be forgotten among the Iban people.

23
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:56,920
After learning pattern-tying (ngebat),

24
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,170
I learn how to do nyungkit,

25
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:00,420
plaiting (beranyam),

26
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:04,570
manggit, embroidering (nyulam), and milih,

27
00:02:04,650 --> 00:02:06,970
I can make them all.

28
00:02:07,350 --> 00:02:09,620
Because I share and teach,

29
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:12,050
because back in those days, I had no schooling.

30
00:02:12,130 --> 00:02:13,130
I had no parents

31
00:02:16,070 --> 00:02:17,670
That is how I learned back then,

32
00:02:17,750 --> 00:02:20,070
so all of those crafts can be done by me.

33
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:23,150
Every single tradition and skill of the Iban people

34
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,920
When I originally make a pua kumbu,

35
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,870
it comes from my dream,

36
00:02:30,950 --> 00:02:35,410
which then enters into my thoughts and mind.

37
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:39,950
I do not copy other people's work, for example,

38
00:02:40,030 --> 00:02:42,670
when I created the pattern Misna Raja Pun Kayu Kaya [Misna, King of the Tree of Wealth]

39
00:02:42,750 --> 00:02:44,200
that was a pua from my dream.

40
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:45,280
After that,

41
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:48,970
I made the Buah Karap Merayong Nanga Panggau [The Karap Pattern Encircling the Mouth of Panggau]

42
00:02:49,050 --> 00:02:51,870
That is another one of my dream pua—it cannot be matched,

43
00:02:51,950 --> 00:02:55,890
and its name is very long, so that is all I will say about it.

44
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:03,660
So, when I first became a leader among the Pua Kumbu weavers

45
00:03:03,750 --> 00:03:08,050
at the very beginning back then, because I was heading [the local chapter of] Saberkas

46
00:03:08,130 --> 00:03:13,670
I brought together all the women of our longhouse to tie patterns (ngebat).

47
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:18,320
Because pattern-tying wasn't just like how people did it in the past;

48
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:21,320
I told them it could become a source of income.

49
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:26,470
So all the women in our longhouse joined in to tie patterns.

50
00:03:26,550 --> 00:03:31,090
Then throughout all of Sungai Kain, Kapit,

51
00:03:31,170 --> 00:03:33,320
whoever could tie patterns started doing so

52
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:35,000
That was my intention back then,

53
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:37,630
so that this traditional heritage would not die out

54
00:03:37,710 --> 00:03:41,170
Up until now, because back then

55
00:03:41,250 --> 00:03:48,050
many people bought directly from us in the longhouse

56
00:03:48,130 --> 00:03:49,980
whenever buyers came looking for pua.

57
00:03:50,060 --> 00:03:52,820
That remains the target of my heart's intention,

58
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:53,950
right up until today.

59
00:03:54,430 --> 00:03:57,970
And then I went on to perform ngar [the mordanting/oil-treatment ritual].

60
00:03:58,050 --> 00:04:01,870
In the past, I didn't want to perform ngar. Ngar is difficult and exhausting.

61
00:04:01,950 --> 00:04:04,900
But I had to, because otherwise it wouldn't work out

62
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:08,080
people don't really want to buy chemical-dyed pua (pua selup).

63
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:09,720
If it isn't treated with ngar,

64
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:12,450
the natural engkudu red dye won't bind or take color.

65
00:04:12,850 --> 00:04:17,770
So for that yellow color, that comes from my own discovery.

66
00:04:18,250 --> 00:04:21,170
Because when we were working during the farming season,

67
00:04:21,250 --> 00:04:24,170
I saw that wood was yellow, so I harvested it.

68
00:04:24,250 --> 00:04:26,900
That became what people call the akar penawar landak.

69
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:30,970
Why it is called akar penawar landak: when a porcupine has eaten [poisonous plants/fruit],

70
00:04:31,050 --> 00:04:35,630
it eats that root - that is why our ancestors back then called it as that.

71
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:40,970
So that is what we have been using right up until today, just like engkudu.

72
00:04:41,050 --> 00:04:47,030
That yellow one uses the porcupine antidote root (akar penawar landak).

73
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:54,520
That blue one, or what we call dark brown or black (celum), uses renggat or tarum.

74
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:57,520
And that red one uses engkudu.

75
00:04:57,600 --> 00:05:01,620
As for my pua, they can be washed.

76
00:05:01,700 --> 00:05:05,100
Nowadays you can even soak them and they will hold up.

77
00:05:05,650 --> 00:05:10,870
If its engkudu Pua for me, then it is made from engkudu.

78
00:05:10,950 --> 00:05:16,236
But if you mix it, of course it will turn out different.

79
00:05:16,316 --> 00:05:22,750
I would not be able to say for everyone else, I wouldn't want to lie.

80
00:05:25,950 --> 00:05:30,570
So I was appointed as Adiguru [Master Craftsman] back then

81
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:36,470
After I became Adiguru, I was designated  Tokoh Kraf Negara [National Craft Icon]

82
00:05:36,550 --> 00:05:41,970
So I am truly happy

83
00:05:42,050 --> 00:05:44,970
- to everyone from Kraftangan Malaysia,

84
00:05:45,050 --> 00:05:47,970
I am so pleased with them,

85
00:05:48,050 --> 00:05:52,820
because they all have

86
00:05:52,900 --> 00:05:57,220
given me recognition to strengthen my motivation,

87
00:05:57,300 --> 00:06:03,750
encouraging me to teach all the women and men too

88
00:06:03,830 --> 00:06:06,970
there is nothing wrong with men learning if they wish to learn.

89
00:06:07,050 --> 00:06:09,720
In the evenings after completing all their daily work.

90
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:12,070
That is what brings joy to my heart.

91
00:06:12,150 --> 00:06:16,650
To the point that I am so happy to be invited here by all of you like this, my children.

92
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:20,620
My heart is at peace and filled with joy,

93
00:06:20,700 --> 00:06:22,040
even as I have grown old

94
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:26,770
From when I was still 40 years old back then,

95
00:06:26,850 --> 00:06:31,030
right up until my 80s now.

96
00:06:34,550 --> 00:06:37,470
So these are my words of advice,

97
00:06:37,550 --> 00:06:39,890
because I am indeed old now.

98
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:46,770
So I hope that whoever - it is my heart's wish

99
00:06:46,850 --> 00:06:52,420
that they will take my place in the future to lead and guide all of our people

100
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:57,200
My grandchildren, my children, my lineage

101
00:06:58,250 --> 00:07:02,650
Everyone who wants to craft these textiles, I ask them to succeed me,

102
00:07:02,730 --> 00:07:04,580
because I may pass away in the future

103
00:07:04,660 --> 00:07:08,520
so that there will be someone to take my place.

104
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:11,520
Crafts like weaving, like pattern-tying (ngebat).

105
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:14,520
As for ngar, if we do not receive a dream,

106
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:17,520
we cannot easily perform ngar.

107
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:20,520
Those sacred practices cannot be treated carelessly.

108
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:23,520
That is my heart's deepest wish: I am overjoyed

109
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:26,520
and I hope that others will carry this continuity forward

110
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:32,120
, so that the work of our women will never become extinct

111
00:07:32,200 --> 00:07:38,170
so that what has been held and preserved for so long will help enhance our living standards

112
00:07:38,250 --> 00:07:43,270
increase our household income, and assist our husbands and sons.

113
00:07:43,350 --> 00:07:46,350
That is what has been in my thoughts.
